# Overview
#
# Reports `not only` and `not just`, mostly in the paired form `not only
# X but Y` (`the cache holds not only rules but results`). The paired
# form presents two facts as a contrast, so the reader has to work out
# that both hold. The fix makes the direct claim (`the cache holds rules
# and results`) and gives a needed contrast its own sentence. The prose
# skill bans the paired form. The rule sits at Recommended, because an
# unpaired `not just` also states a correction that the reader needs.
# The rule passes that form only after a comma or an open paren.
#
# Expression
#
# `which` holds `only` and `just`. The first branch reports `not only`
# or `not just` unless a comma or an open paren comes before it. Weir
# has no lookbehind, so the branch consumes the token before the space
# ahead of `not`, and the filter narrows the finding back to the phrase.
# A comma fails that token, and an open paren leaves no space before
# `not`.
#
# The first branch needs a token before `not`, so a sentence that opens
# with `Not only` counts through the second branch or the third. The
# second branch needs the helping verb that this form moves ahead of the
# subject (`Not only does the cache hold rules`).
#
# The third branch finds the paired form anywhere, after a comma too, by
# matching `but` up to eight words after `not only` or `not just`. A
# list picks its longest match and never backtracks, so a gap list
# followed by `but` swallows the `but`. Each gap length is therefore its
# own alternative that ends in `but`. The last word before `but` can
# carry a comma (`Not only the cache, but the log grows`).
#
# `isn't just` and `more than just` make the same move (`it isn't just a
# cache`, `it is more than just a cache`), so the last two branches
# report them.
#
# False Positives
#
# A `not just` or a `not only` after a comma or an open paren passes.
# That form is a trailing correction (`Name the log events, not just the
# sentinel`, `(not just the prune)`), and it names a contrast the reader
# needs.
#
# Without the comma or the paren, the same correction still matches
# (`Name the log events and not just the sentinel`). The rule accepts
# that misfire.
#
# `not just !any` skips the idiom `not just any file`. The glued hyphen
# in `not just-in-time` already fails the whitespace match.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseJust skips `just` after a negation and after `than`, so only this
# rule reports `not just` and `more than just`. ProseNegativeContraction
# also reports the contraction in `isn't just`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 31 lines in the measure.py corpus, and in 20 of 20
# sampled hits the direct claim said the same. Of the 20, 13 were `not
# only` or `not just`, 5 were `more than just`, and 2 were `isn't just`
# or `aren't just`.
expr aux [does, do, did, is, are, was, were, can, could, will, would, has, have, should, must]
expr which [only, (just !any)]
expr last [*, (*,)]
expr tail [(@last but), (* @last but), (* * @last but), (* * * @last but), (* * * * @last but), (* * * * * @last but), (* * * * * * @last but), (* * * * * * * @last but)]
expr main [<(!(,)( )not @which), (not @which)>, (not only @aux), (not [only, just] @tail), ([isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't] just), (more than just)]
let message "`not only` or `not just`. Make the direct claim, and give a needed contrast its own sentence."
let description "Flags `not only` and `not just` constructions, except an unpaired correction after a comma or an open paren."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The cache holds rules and results."
allows "Not just any file can be opened this way."
allows "The runtime uses ahead-of-time compilation, not just-in-time."
allows "Name the log events, not just the sentinel."
allows "Refusing the run (not just the prune) also prevents the race."
